---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const t = {
  title: "History",
  desc: "How Neonatox was born: from curiosity to building an operating system from scratch.",
  backHome: "← Back to home"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={t.title} description={t.desc} lang="en">
  <section class="py-16 px-4">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <a href="/en/" class="text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-700 mb-8 inline-block">{t.backHome}</a>

      <article class="space-y-12">

        <!-- INTRO -->
        <div class="text-center">
          <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-400 to-purple-500 mb-8">{t.title}</h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            NeonatoX wasn't born because Linux was broken.<br />
            <strong>It was born because I truly wanted to understand it.</strong>
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            For many people, free software is just "using free stuff." For me, it always meant something deeper:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-600 space-y-1 mt-4 max-w-md mx-auto">
            <li>The freedom to modify.</li>
            <li>The freedom to build.</li>
            <li>The freedom to understand what's really happening inside the machine.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mt-6">
            I didn't want to install a distribution. I wanted to <strong>experience</strong> those freedoms firsthand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 mb-4">
          <img src="/screenshots/historia-hero.jpg" alt="Neonatox - The beginning" class="w-full h-auto" loading="lazy">
        </div>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- THE BEGINNING: FROM VEGNUX TO NEONATOX -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">The beginning: From VegnuX to NeonatoX</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">
            It all formally began in <strong>2007</strong> with the publication of the <em>Manifesto 0.1.0</em> of the <strong>MKL/VegnuX</strong> project (Venezuelan GNU/Linux eXperimental Project). This was the first initiative to develop its own distribution, guided by the principles of free software, source code availability, and experimentation.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            Over time, the project's vision evolved. <strong>VegnuX</strong> became the "banner" or umbrella for a broader set of free software initiatives and projects. Within this growing ecosystem, the distribution itself needed its own distinct identity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            Everything changed the day I discovered <strong>Linux From Scratch</strong>. At first it was just copy-pasting commands. But at some point, it clicked. I stopped following instructions and started <strong>understanding</strong> why things were done that way: the toolchain, glibc, the kernel, the paths, the environment variables…
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            That was the moment Neonatox stopped being just a crazy idea.
          </p>
          <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 mt-6">
            <img src="/screenshots/historia-comienzo.jpg" alt="Linux From Scratch - The beginning" class="w-full h-auto" loading="lazy">
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- ORIGIN OF THE NAME AND THE TRANSITION -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Origin of the name and the transition</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">
            It was in this context that <strong>NeonatoX</strong> was born. While VegnuX covered more general projects, NeonatoX consolidated itself as the specific name of the distribution. The first official milestone under this new identity was the release of <strong>NeonatoX Cañaverales 0.48.0</strong> in <strong>April 2009</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            This name change not only marked a new stage of maturity for the distro, but also helped clearly differentiate it from other local initiatives with phonetically similar names (such as Venenux), consolidating its own memorable identity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            The name <strong>Neonatox</strong> came up in a conversation with my brother. I was explaining that I wasn't modifying any distro, but creating something from scratch. Then he said:
          </p>
          <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-cyan-500 pl-4 italic text-gray-500 my-6 text-lg">
            "It's like a neonate… but with an X" → <strong class="text-cyan-400">Neonatox</strong>
          </blockquote>
          <p class="text-gray-600">A system being born from scratch, under the wing of the VegnuX project, but with its own personality.</p>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- FIRST VERSIONS -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">First versions</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">The first versions were a beautiful disaster. No package manager, everything compiled by hand. I especially remember compiling <strong>XFree86</strong>… it was traumatic.</p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">That experience made it clear I needed modularity, reproducibility, and real control.</p>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- NHOPKG AND THE COMMUNITY -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">nhopkg and the community</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">Then came one of the project's pillars: <strong>nhopkg</strong>.</p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            Originally created by <strong>Jaime Gil</strong> for his project <strong>Nhoax</strong>, this package manager was simple, understandable, and adaptable. When Jaime left the project, I decided to continue it and adapt it to Neonatox.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            Development was transparent and iterative. Historical records show how it went from a manager that "resolved some dependencies" to the creation of <strong>pynhopkg</strong> (a Python tool for managing package sources), thanks to community collaboration. Developers such as <em>dominuskernel</em> and <em>Nehemías Velázquez</em> contributed to the installer and the manager, demonstrating the community spirit of free software.
          </p>
          <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 mt-6">
            <img src="/screenshots/historia-nhopkg.jpg" alt="nhopkg - The package manager" class="w-full h-auto" loading="lazy">
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- THE PAUSE -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">The pause</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">Like almost all personal projects, Neonatox also had its winter. Years where real life won. The project froze, not from lack of desire, but because it simply wasn't the right time.</p>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- THE RETURN -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">The return (2023)</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">When I came back, I did it with a different mindset. With more experience and clarity. What was once a messy experiment began to turn into something more serious and structured.</p>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- PHILOSOPHY AND PRAGMATISM -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Philosophy and pragmatism</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600">
            At the beginning of the project, the vision was strict: using <strong>linux-libre</strong> as the only kernel option, maintaining absolute purity of 100% free software. It was an invaluable learning stage and a principle we will always respect.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            However, over the years and with the rapid evolution of hardware, reality imposed itself. Keeping a system usable as a daily-use machine without proprietary firmware (needed for Wi-Fi, modern graphics, or certain peripherals) became unfeasible, even for me as a developer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            NeonatoX evolved toward a more <strong>pragmatic</strong> approach, but without betraying its roots. It's not about abandoning the ideals of free software, but balancing them with technical reality. <strong>Anyone who wishes to keep a 100% free system still has that option</strong>: the free kernel, along with its sources and binaries, remains completely available in our repositories.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
            Today, the project seeks to offer a system that truly works in the real world, being fully transparent and giving the user total control to decide what they install on their own machine.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- TODAY -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Today</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600"><strong>NeonatoX is, above all, a puzzle.</strong></p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">It's not just another distribution. It's a platform to learn, break things, rebuild them, and understand how an operating system really works.</p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-6">And it's still here. It wasn't abandoned.<br /><strong class="text-cyan-400">It was just waiting for the right moment.</strong></p>
          <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 mt-6">
            <img src="/screenshots/historia-hoy.jpg" alt="Neonatox today" class="w-full h-auto" loading="lazy">
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-gray-200" />

        <!-- HISTORICAL REFERENCES -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Historical references</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-600 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <a href="https://vegnux.wordpress.com/2007/05/29/lanzamiento-del-manifiesto-0-1-0-mklvegnux/" class="text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-700" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                MKL/VegnuX Manifesto 0.1.0 (2007)
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://vegnux.wordpress.com/2009/04/16/liberada-neonatox-canaverales-0-48-0/" class="text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-700" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Release of NeonatoX Cañaverales 0.48.0 (2009)
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://vegnux.wordpress.com/2013/05/28/version-actualizada-de-neonatox-06-2rc5/" class="text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-700" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Evolution and transparency of nhopkg / pynhopkg (2013)
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://vegnux.wordpress.com/" class="text-cyan-500 hover:text-cyan-700" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Official blog of the VegnuX Project
              </a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>